Changes to code inside functions that remain (sync them by hand):
  ftp.c     ftp_stat(): the SIZE reply is parsed with the overflow check that
            http_parse_num() in http.c uses, so a value above LLONG_MAX is a
            protocol error and reads as "size unknown", instead of wrapping
            to a negative or small size.  FreeBSD has the unchecked loop.
  http.c    http_request_body(): a chunked response ignores Content-Length, as
            RFC 9112 6.3 says, instead of cutting the decoded body off at it.
            FreeBSD stops at Content-Length and exits 0 with a short body.
  main.c    fetch(): at the end of the data the stream is closed and a failed
            close is a failed transfer.  An FTP server's final status (226,
            426, 451...) is read by that close, so when the size is unknown a
            server that aborted the transfer is no longer reported as success.
            FreeBSD ignores the status and exits 0 with a partial file.
  common.c  fetch_read_by(): the "len -= rlen; if (len == 0) break;" after a
            successful read is gone; the next statement breaks anyway.
  http.c    hdr_error and its two "case hdr_error:" branches are gone;
            http_next_header() never returned it.  hdr_syserror is now -1.
  common.c  Timeouts use the monotonic clock as in fbb0b7e, through the same
            mstime_mono(), and fetch_connect_sd(), fetch_accept_sd(),
            fetch_tls_handshake(), fetch_read_by() and fetch_read_exact(),
            which are ours, use it too.  The deadline is computed in
            fetch_deadline() with the product in an unsigned long, and the
            wait handed to poll() is clamped to INT_MAX, as the old
            fetch_deltams() did for struct timeval.
  common.c  fetch_ssl(): the server name passed to libtls has the brackets of
            an IPv6 literal removed.  The URL parser keeps them in the host,
            and libtls rejected "[::1]" as a server name, so https://[::1]/
            never connected.  (libtls only; FreeBSD's OpenSSL code has the
            same bracketed host in fetch_ssl_verify_hname() and never
            verifies an IP6 SAN.)

Fixed here, still wrong in FreeBSD (candidates to report; drop the note if a
later import has the fix):
  main.c    the long option --passive-portrange-default returns 'T' (the -T
            timeout, which takes an argument) instead of 'U'.  On FreeBSD
            15.1 it crashes in strtol(NULL): exit 139.  Here it returns 'U'.
  common.c  fetch_socks5_init(): the port is written as two bytes in network
            order.  FreeBSD does htons() and then stores the low byte first,
            which is right only on a little-endian host (70af989).  (Fix
            drafted for FreeBSD, not yet sent.)
  common.c  fetch_socks5_getenv(): without a port, "host" is copied with
            strdup().  FreeBSD calls strndup(s, ext - s) with ext NULL, a
            subtraction from a null pointer.  (Fix drafted for FreeBSD, not
            yet sent.)
  common.c  fetch_socks5_init(): the replies are read exactly, with
            fetch_read_exact(): VER and METHOD (2 bytes), then the 4-byte
            header, then BND.ADDR as long as ATYP says (a domain name has a
            length byte) and BND.PORT.  RSV must be zero and an unknown ATYP
            is refused.  FreeBSD reads BUFF_SIZE bytes in one fetch_read(),
            which can swallow the start of the application protocol or stop
            short of the reply, and does not check RSV.

Changes to code inside functions that remain, continued:
  common.c  fetch_connect(): the connect and the wait for it moved into
            fetch_connect_sd() (ours), which also asks SO_ERROR for the
            outcome instead of reporting ECONNREFUSED for any POLLOUT that
            was not exactly POLLOUT.  The FTP passive data connection uses it
            too, so -T covers it.
  common.c  fetch_resolve(): strchrnul() is hb + strcspn(hb, ":").
  common.c  fetch_reopen(): SO_NOSIGPIPE is not set, since
            OpenBSD has none; main.c ignores SIGPIPE, so a closed peer gives
            EPIPE.
  common.c  fetch_no_proxy_match(): an entry matches the host and the hosts
            below it ("example.com" matches www.example.com, not
            notexample.com); a leading "." or "*." asks for subdomains only.
            FreeBSD matches by suffix, so "example.com" matches
            notexample.com.
  common.c  fetch_read_word(), netrc: the lookup of the path is in
            fetch_netrc_path(), which caches the answer; main.c calls it
            before pledge(2), when getpwuid() no longer works.
  common.c  fetch_info() and fetch_debug() (new; common.h DEBUGF() calls it
            in place of fprintf()): the text goes through fetch_emit(),
            which makes control characters visible with stravis(), because
            it can come from a server or a certificate.
  common.c  fetch_putln(): the debug line is passed through
            fetch_loggable(), which hides PASS, Authorization and
            Proxy-Authorization.
  fetch.c   url debug output: the password is shown as <hidden>.
  ftp.c     ftp_authenticate(): the debug line no longer prints FTP_PASSWORD.
  ftp.c     ftp_transfer(), passive mode: only the port of the PASV or EPSV
            reply is used.  The data connection goes to the host of the
            control connection, so a server cannot point us at another host
            (FTP bounce in reverse).  The memcpy() of the address is gone.
  ftp.c     ftp_transfer(), active mode: the connection is accepted with
            fetch_accept_sd(), from the control connection's peer only, and
            within -T.  FreeBSD calls accept() with no limit.
  ftp.c     ftp_get_proxy(), http.c http_get_proxy(): strcpy() into
            purl->scheme is strlcpy().  http.c http_digest_auth(): sprintf()
            is snprintf().
  http.c    http_new_chunk() returns -1, 0 or 1.  The size is parsed with an
            overflow check and at least one digit, only blanks or an
            extension may follow, an empty line is a closed connection and
            not the last chunk, and the last chunk needs its trailers and
            the empty line.  http_fillbuf() reports EPROTO for a connection
            closed inside a chunk, and for a missing CRLF after the data.
            FreeBSD shifts into a 64-bit size with no check, takes any
            garbage as a size and treats a truncated chunked body as
            complete.  (The report is PR 2544; it is only the missing-final-
            blank-line part.)
  http.c    http_parse_num() (ours) parses Content-Length, Content-Range and
            the sizes in them with an overflow check; http_parse_length() and
            http_parse_range() use it, a range must lie within the object,
            and http_request_body() treats a failure as a protocol error.
            FreeBSD wraps, and ignores the return value.
  http.c    http_request_body(): an error line from the server is shown
            through fetch_redact() when -v is given.
  http.c    http_request_body(): the If-Modified-Since time is built with
            gmtime_r() and strftime(), and the answer is checked; the
            locale_t helper and strptime_l()/strftime_l() are gone since
            OpenBSD has no xlocale.  http_parse_mtime() zeroes struct tm
            first.
  http.c    the debug lines that printed the Digest HA1 and the Basic
            password are gone.
  http.c    the MD5 macros for OpenSSL are gone; <md5.h> is always used.

