Changes in main.c (from usr.bin/fetch/fetch.c; the program is rewritten
around the sandbox and the temporary file, so sync it by hand):
  sandbox   sandbox() is called after the TLS and netrc setup, with pledge(2)
            and unveil(2); see sandbox.c.  -i reads the mtime of its file
            before that, since the file is not unveiled.
  numbers   strtonum() with limits for -B, -S, -T, -w, FTP_TIMEOUT and
            HTTP_TIMEOUT; -B defaults to 64 KiB (FreeBSD 16 KiB); -S has an
            S_flag, so -S 0 means a size of 0.
  options   the SSL options above and b_flag, t_flag, o_flag, i_flag are
            gone; -t only warns.
  output    a transfer is written to a temporary file in the same directory
            (mkstemps, fdopen) and renamed when it is complete, with the
            owner and mode of the file it replaces, never setuid, setgid or
            sticky.  A symlink at the name is replaced.  A device or FIFO is
            opened in place.  With -r or -R a new file keeps its name.
            Writes and the close are checked before the rename.
  -r        the file is opened once (a symlink is followed), and its size is
            taken from that descriptor, replacing the stat()/re-open/compare
            of dev, ino and size.  A read-only file that is complete is not
            an error.
  -a        a retry continues in the open output and asks for the rest;
            if the server starts over, a regular file is truncated, and
            anything else is refused.
  end       at the end of the data the stream is closed and a failure is a
            failed transfer (see above); "may be truncated" and failure_keep
            are gone with the timedout special case.
  text      URLs and server text are printed through shown(), which hides a
            URL password and makes control characters visible.
  misc      SIGPIPE is ignored; siginfo and sigint are sig_atomic_t;
            strcat()/strcpy() are strlcat()/strlcpy(); asprintf() is checked;
            the usage text lists the options that exist.

